M(61,'Which geometric category?',[99,101],'The finite-type objects over a noetherian quasi/pro-schematic object are a catégorie géométrique (?) with the displayed descent and quotient axioms.','Identify the exact category first; a topos or an exact extensive category is a possible reconstruction, not a transcription.','SGA4 IV1.1.2 and Theorem1.2 characterize a topos under explicit axioms including a small generating family and all disjoint universal sums. These extra hypotheses have not been shown for this category.','The pages give a question and conflicting category word, not an account of why the question was abandoned.','Worth a bounded source-recovery run: it would determine what theorem is actually being proposed.',['C_SGA4_IV'])
M(62,'Finite colimits from the proposed axioms',[101],'After base change and sums: donc aux ... finies ???; the precise finite diagrams are uncertain.','In a topos pullback preserves colimits; for a weaker category one must separately establish the needed universal quotient and image properties.','SGA4 IV1.1.2 states universal sums and effective universal equivalence-relation quotients. No exact weaker-axiom implication was found.','The author marks the implication; no historical explanation of its later omission is supplied.','Worth checking once MP61 fixes the category, because the claim can then be tested by a finite categorical argument.',['C_SGA4_IV'])
M(63,'Possible marginal question on inverse-limit subgroups',[102],'The glyph is 2|? beside the subgroup and density discussion; its status as a question is not secure.','For K in a projective limit, K is dense in the limit of its coordinate images; equality requires an additional completeness/closedness condition.','Bourbaki Topologie générale III section7 gives the limit subgroup framework; the cylinder proof is rechecked here. The finite-support subgroup of the product of F2 shows why equality is stronger.','No confirmed open question can be extracted from an undecided glyph.','Worth only a scan check; the written density mathematics is settled.',['C_BourbakiTopology'])
M(64,'Locality of the constructible-intersection notion',[107],'Nature locale de la notion ??, with uncertain name for the local intersection notion.','Patch closedness is local on a patch-open cover; on compact patch basis opens it is equivalent to compactness of each intersection. Abstract local compactness of a subset is not enough.','Stacks08YF Lemmas5.23.2 and5.23.5 support the spectral formulation. The local proof is recorded in narrative section3.','A questioned locality step survives; no source says why it was dropped.','Worth a short source-and-hypothesis concordance, because this local notion feeds the later constructible categories.',['C_Stacks08YF'])
M(65,'Two disputed hypothesis flags',[108,110],'Pro reads too restrictive on108 and les vérifier on110; Codex reads always verified and sont vérifiées.','Every scheme morphism pulls back retrocompact opens and constructible subsets; qc maps need not send constructibles to constructibles.','Stacks054H Lemma29.23.1 proves pullback; Lemma29.23.2 and Theorem29.23.3 impose finite-presentation/type conditions for images. The generic singleton gives the direct negative test.','These may not be authorial doubts at all. The independent text reverses both readings.','Worth high-priority ink checking; do not open a new research problem on the basis of the Pro wording.',['C_Stacks054H'])
M(66,'Néron-spaces programme heading',[118,124],'Pro reads Pb à poser. Espaces de Néron!; Codex allows pts épais. Espaces de Néron. No detailed question is stated in this heading.','Use126–132 to recover the thick-point programme; do not infer a separate theorem from the title alone.','Greenberg1966 Theorem1 and BG18.2 give approximation and point realization, but neither supplies the omitted definition of a Néron space in this programme.','No specific unresolved proposition or reason for dropping it appears in the title.','Worth retaining as programme provenance; mathematical work should start from MP67–71.',['C_Greenberg1966','C_BG'])
M(67,'Equivalence of the thickness conditions',[126,128],'The four (Ép) conditions and arrows are written with questions; one arrow is labelled M.L. et I=N.','Under the precise source definitions, (Ép)′ implies genericity (Ép). Under noetherian stage hypotheses, constructibility of closure{x} is equivalent to eventual pullback of closure{x_i}. The complete four-way equivalence is not established.','Stacks0A2Y proves constructible descent in spectral inverse limits. Stacks0597 proves nonempty limits of countable nonempty ML set systems. Neither by itself identifies the missing scheme-level ML hypothesis.','The visible question marks and missing hypotheses explain the present identification obstruction; no later historical reason for abandonment was found.','Worth a focused run: formulate the exact fibre/image ML condition and test the missing arrow against noetherian affine examples.',['C_Stacks0A2Y','C_Stacks0597'])
M(68,'The undefined right-hand thickness implication',[128],'The arrow near ??? introduces X′_i and an inverse-image equality not defined on this leaf.','No strongest mathematical statement can responsibly be selected before X′_i and the arrow are read; possible closure and image interpretations must remain alternatives.','The spectral descent and ML results above are comparators only. Greenberg/Sebag searches did not recover this notation.','The question is explicit but its operands are not secure.','Worth recovering from the scans before proof work; otherwise different statements would be conflated.',['C_Stacks0A2Y','C_Stacks0597','C_Sebag'])
M(69,'Local finite equations for a thick closure',[128],'The local prime-ideal fragment q⊃p iff q⊃f1...fn is abbreviated and interrupted.','On D(g), require sqrt(pA_g)=sqrt((f1,...,fn)A_g); the f_i are a list of equations, not necessarily a product.','Constructible closed subsets of an affine spectral space have quasi-compact open complement and hence finitely generated radical defining ideals; this is the finite Boolean mechanism in Stacks04ZC and0A2Y.','The fragment has no completed arrow or explicit research question; retain as an unfinished proof step.','Worth a short reconstruction after MP68, because it can make the thickness criterion algebraically testable.',['C_Stacks04ZC','C_Stacks0A2Y'])
M(70,'Greenberg thick points and finite-index maps',[131],'Proposition1 asserts existence, thick image, k(y)⊂k(x), and a finite-index promorphism near the closure for a dominant generically separable rational map.','The valuative set map exists where the generic map is defined and the target is proper. Preservation of the unidentified thick notion, field descent and finite-index morphicity require separate hypotheses.','BGv6 Proposition18.2 supplies complete-DVR point realization. Stacks0BX5 gives unique proper extension. Sebag preprint7.2.1–2 gives a bounded-Jacobian cylinder comparison with smoothness and dimension hypotheses. No exact finite-index claim was located.','No proof or definition of finite index is present here; sources do not say why the author left the assertion unproved.','Worth substantial source recovery before a mathematical run: the present field-perfectness distinction is essential.',['C_BG','C_StacksProper','C_Sebag'])
M(71,'Bounded simple pieces and constructible Greenberg images',[132],'Proposition2 uses X_K-simple subsets and a bounded ell|d invariant to obtain constructible image and a finite morphic partition.','A bounded-Jacobian/defect cylinder image theorem is true in the explicit smooth-source framework of Sebag; identification with the page’s simple/ell/index conditions remains unproved.','Greenberg1966 Theorem1 gives linear approximation; Sebag preprint4.3.12 gives constructible finite-level cylinder images and7.2.2 gives a cylinder-image result with bounded data. They do not settle the unexpanded manuscript quantifiers.','The sheet is an assertion, not a securely marked conjecture; missing definitions prevent an exact fate assignment.','Worth a high-interest run after definition recovery, not a broad claim that motivic integration settled it.',['C_Greenberg1966','C_Sebag','C_BG'])
M(72,'Laurent ind-scheme notation and strict stages',[134,135],'The explicit caution is abus de langage ici!; the nonisomorphism sentence has n′≥n, which fails at equality.','The coefficient functor is an ind-affine group; for strict n′>n its fixed-valuation stage inclusions are not isomorphisms.','Osipov–Zhu Lemma2.1 gives Laurent-unit factorization. This run proves the precise bounded-stage assertions by coordinate equations and dual-number witnesses.','The caution concerns notation. The equality boundary is a transcription/statement issue, not evidence of a surviving open theorem.','Worth an ink check and a clean source commentary; no further proof-development task is required for the checked assertions.',['C_OsipovZhu'])
M(73,'Pro-scheme Hom with only quasi-compact sources',[136],'The comparison is asserted with finite presentation for target stages and a quasi-compact insertion for source stages.','The comparison is established for qcqs source stages and affine transition maps; the broader qc-only source wording is not certified here.','Stacks01ZC Proposition32.6.1 explicitly uses quasi-separatedness to descend gluing equalities; EGAIV8.13.1 is the historical comparator.','The page provides no explanation whether quasi-separatedness was implicit or overlooked.','Worth a short convention audit before seeking counterexamples to the broader reading.',['C_Stacks01ZC','C_EGAIV2'])
M(74,'Which group of nilpotent coordinate changes?',[138,140,142],'The sheets switch between evaluation on a nilpotent ideal, an augmented jet algebra, coefficient parameters and an undefined tangent module.','Augmentation-preserving truncated-algebra automorphisms require zero constant term and unit linear determinant. Nilpotent translations give bijections on m^n but need not preserve the naive polynomial truncation ideal.','Stacks00UP gives formal étale lifting; Stacks04FJ gives the lift torsor. The two earlier-run counterexamples and full quadratic composition are rerun here.','The calculation is unfinished and its ambient object is not fixed; no source records a reason for abandonment.','Worth a precise functor-of-points formulation, limited to the source’s finite nilpotent level before generalization.',['C_Stacks00UP','C_Stacks04FJ'])
M(75,'Withdrawn formulations',[101,109,116],'The epimorphism–monomorphism factorization item, an upper corollary, and the entire compactness draft are cancelled on their respective leaves.','These are source-history entries, not live open mathematical problems. Surviving replacements are tracked separately in claims402,417,422,430.','SGA4 IV and Olivier give comparisons for the surviving material, but they do not reveal the author’s reason for these particular cancellations.','Only same-sheet withdrawal is evidenced; archive order does not date the revisions.','Worth retaining for the companion commentary; do not assign new research work to cancelled claims.',['C_SGA4_IV','C_OlivierGlobal'])
M(76,'Scope of the isolated Lie-cohomology exercise',[127],'An exercise concludes H^p(g,M)=0 using an invertible operator T=dρ+ρd, but its representation-theoretic antecedents lie elsewhere.','If T is an invertible chain map homotopic to zero, the complex is contractible via T^−1ρ. This does not imply unrestricted Lie-cohomology vanishing.','Bourbaki LieI exercise context is a publication comparator; the exact omitted antecedents were not recovered from an accessible full edition. The chain-homotopy lemma is directly proved.','Missing reused pages, not evidence of a new open conjecture.','Worth only a source-concordance task if this exercise is used in the archive commentary.',['C_BourbakiLie'])
